   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "841e626c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3 Relative Prices and Demand"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6dcac2c9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Solving the model for p3, where p3 differs from 0.5 to 3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 51,
   "id": "08c7c220",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1100x800 with 4 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "p3_vec = np.linspace(0.5, 3.0, 30)\n",
    "\n",
    "results = {}\n",
    "for sigma_B, label in [(0.40, 'complements'), (3.00, 'substitutes')]:\n",
    "    model = ConsumerClass(par={'sigma_B': sigma_B})\n",
    "    s1s, s2s, s3s, x1s, x2s, x3s = [], [], [], [], [], []\n",
    "    for p3 in p3_vec:\n",
    "        model.par.p3 = p3\n",
    "        opt = model.solve(do_print=False)\n",
    "        x1, x2, x3 = model.quantities(opt.s1, opt.w)\n",
    "        s1s.append(opt.s1); s2s.append(opt.s2); s3s.append(opt.s3)\n",
    "        x1s.append(x1); x2s.append(x2); x3s.append(x3)\n",
    "    results[label] = dict(s1=s1s, s2=s2s, s3=s3s, x1=x1s, x2=x2s, x3=x3s)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(11,8))\n",
    "for label in results:\n",
    "    axes[0,0].plot(p3_vec, results[label]['s2'], label=f'bus ({label})')\n",
    "    axes[0,1].plot(p3_vec, results[label]['s3'], label=f'train ({label})')\n",
    "    axes[1,0].plot(p3_vec, results[label]['x2'], label=f'bus ({label})')\n",
    "    axes[1,1].plot(p3_vec, results[label]['x3'], label=f'train ({label})')\n",
    "for ax, title in zip(axes.flat, ['bus share','train share','bus quantity','train quantity']):\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel('$p_3$'); ax.set_title(title); ax.legend()\n",
    "fig.tight_layout()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "05d3cd7b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Discussion\n",
    "\n",
    "When the bus and train are complements, the consumer can't switch to more bus trips to make up for the increases in train prices. Thus, increasing p3 increases the train's budget share while decreasing the quantity of train trips taken, showing that train demand is relatively inelastic because the quantity decrease doesn't perfectly offset the higher price. The number of bus trips also falls over the same range.\n",
    "\n",
    "When the bus and train are substitutes, the consumer is able to switch between transportation modes and the train's budget share falls more to compensate for the increase in p3.\n",
    "\n",
    "Food consumption (x1) barely moves in either calibration, in agreement with the nested structure of the model where price changes in train tickets primarily impacts the allocation of spending within the travel goods nest."
   ]
  },
